 pricing in of people  thinking 50 basis points in march uh a  full percentage point like what's your  take on how many and how severe uh and  at this point can they ignore it anymore  like do they have to hike interest rates  after a cpi print like this  well let me start by saying uh the funny  joke in the room right now is that  they're still buying bonds  the fed is still doing qe right now  i mean it's obviously not funny it's a  disaster but uh so yeah you made a great  point earlier right about um  you know  once the rich people start losing money  then they'll phone up the fed to fun of  the treasury and say hey look guys  you're this inflation's a problem now at  first right like we have record  corporate um operating margins here in  the us if you look at the s p 500  constituents and part of the reason we  have racket corporate operating margins  is because all this inflation that us  consumers and businesses are eating is  going straight to the bottom line of  these major corporations who are  obviously in bed with wall street who  are obviously the further in bed with  with the folks down in dc so you know  there's they're laughing to the bank and  this is why you've seen a record  earnings bonanza that's why you see  again record operating margins but there  comes to be a point where it becomes a  political problem right um at some point  it's a political problem for the people  who run the banks the people who run  these companies when their workers are  showing up saying hey look i'm out of  here if you don't pay me xyz and once  that all that starts to happen in mass  and we're obviously seeing that in the  most recent jobs report on  last friday we got the average hourly  earnings statistic jumped up to nine  percent on the season adjusted  annualized uh rate of change basis  that's the number fastest number we've  seen since um since the in a year but  the employment cost index which measures  everything that you have to pay your  employees to to 